==Central Franconian==

===Etymology===
* {{IPAchar|/a/}} is from Middle High German {{m|gmh||a}} in most closed syllables.
* {{IPAchar|/aː/}} is from {{m|gmh||a}} before certain clusters, in most dialects also in open syllables; in southern Moselle Franconian from ''ou''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|gmw-cfr|qual1=short|/a/|qual2=long|/aː/}}

===Letter===
{{head|gmw-cfr|letters}}

# {{ngd|A letter in the German-based alphabet of Central Franconian.}}
# {{ngd|A letter in the Dutch-based alphabet of Central Franconian.}}

====Usage notes====
{{rel-top|Doubling of long A}}
* In the German-based spelling, long ''a'' is doubled to ''aa'' when the German cognate word has ''aa'' as well. Long ''a'' may or may not be doubled in the following cases:
** when it is followed by two or more consonants: {{m|gmw-cfr|Saach}}, {{q|rarely}} {{m|gmw-cfr|Sach}};
** when the German cognate has two vowel letters: {{m|gmw-cfr|glaawe}}, {{m|gmw-cfr|glawe}} (German {{m|de|glauben}});
** when the German cognate has a consonant lost or not present in Central Franconian: {{m|gmw-cfr|waade}}, {{m|gmw-cfr|wade}} (German {{m|de|warten}});
** when the German cognate has a short vowel: {{m|gmw-cfr|baal}}, {{m|gmw-cfr|bal}} (German {{m|de|bald}}).
* In the Dutch-based spelling, long ''a'' is always doubled in closed syllables, always written simple in open syllables.
{{rel-bottom}}

